export const delivery = [
  { id: 'Q01', title: 'Complete deterministic regression coverage', goal: 'Verify every lesson and learning feature without billed work.', prompt: `Complete the Node offline suites, lesson/source audits, guide/recording/infographic integrity checks, milestone references and Playwright interaction/UI checks for all 75 lessons. Ordinary tests use authored fixtures or injected ports and disable all billed/provider/external-notification paths. Test failures as behaviors: denial, paused/incomplete outcomes, ownership, cancellation, cleanup uncertainty, malformed input, unknown cost and test-gaming rejection must remain explicit.
Run typecheck, applicable tests, audit:lesson-guides, narration:check, infographics:check, code:audit and build. Check every real image view and committed media playback; a synthetic time event alone does not prove an MP3 loads. Freeze expected feature counts at 75 lessons, 525 guide MP3s, 150 PNGs, 150 visual MP3s, 75 code MP3s, 498 code sections and 900 image regions. Counts of tests are observations, not values to fabricate or hard-code to obtain a pass. Fix regressions without deleting assertions, adding broad retries or treating timeout as success. Report exactly what ran and remaining prerequisites.` },
  { id: 'Q02', title: 'Harden Windows browser and worker supervision', goal: 'Preserve meaningful failures while handling known startup and capture conditions.', prompt: `Verify Windows workspace aliases, realpath source watching and private Vite filesystem exclusions. Before browser scenarios start, launch an owned temporary headless Chrome page and await an actual visible lesson heading under a bounded startup deadline. Then close it and run Playwright with one worker per supervised backend/frontend pair. Use 60-second ordinary scenario budgets with normal assertion deadlines and only explicitly bounded longer operations.
For the Lab 64 MCP screenshot flow preserve bounded text-only diagnostics. Retry only the exact read-only Page.captureScreenshot 'Unable to capture screenshot' error once, foregrounding the same page; do not retry navigation/form submission or unrelated errors. Validate the returned PNG and use it as proof. For Lab 70 use bounded isolated Python startup/readiness, fixed arguments, no shell and truthful remote cleanup distinction. Reject sources under server/acceptance with HTTP 403; that denial is an expected boundary, not a test to weaken. Close all owned processes. Reproduce an actual failure before changing its bound; test both permitted recovery and exhaustion.` },
  { id: 'Q03', title: 'Build a sanitized reproducible source package', goal: 'Distribute authored code and included media without private state.', prompt: `Implement a strict authored-source ZIP allowlist and SHA-256 distribution manifest. Include package/lock/config files, src/shared/server, scripts/tests/browser-tests, fixtures, guides, public recorded media, infographic prompts and the student prompt workbook/reference data. Reject symlinks and exclude .env except .env.example, local resource pins, keys, browser profiles, private provider evidence, logs, runtime repositories, node_modules and build output. A study workspace must not be recursively deleted to sanitize it.
Run typecheck/build first, then credential/path scans, then package:course sequentially so scanning never races a changing dist directory. Scan tracked files and actual npm pack inventory, not just .gitignore. Verify ZIP members/CRCs and every manifest hash, plus all 525/150 recordings and 150 PNGs. If publishing supplied assets, confirm matching catalog text, IDs, images, timings and file hashes. Produce checksums and a short start guide with Node/Git prerequisites, npm ci, preserved .env.example setup, printed Vite URL, offline baseline, notebook save and backup. Never include a learner's notes or an author's secret settings.` },
  { id: 'Q04', title: 'Run parallel GitHub regression with a final gate', goal: 'Retain every check while shortening hosted wall-clock time.', prompt: `Create .github/workflows/regression.yml for push to main, pull requests and workflow_dispatch. Use immutable verified GitHub Action commits, contents:read and checkout persist-credentials:false. Root env disables Managed live mode, narration synthesis, external notifications and remote telemetry. Use Windows runners and Node 24; unit/browser jobs explicitly install Chrome and isolated pinned Python requirements. Do not pass provider secrets to automatic jobs.
Run offline checks in one job and the complete Playwright suite in a two-job matrix with --shard=1/2 and --shard=2/2. Keep workers:1 within each job, separate owned servers and fail-fast:false so independent diagnostics survive. List all tests and both shards first; prove the union equals the original suite exactly with zero duplicates/missing tests. Save failure-context Markdown under unique shard artifact names.
Preserve a final job/check named offline that needs both the checks job and browser matrix. Its !cancelled() condition must allow it to report failure after a prerequisite fails. Explicitly reject any needs result other than success before checkout/build/scan/package/upload. Only every-success may upload authored-course-commitSHA. Give bounded per-job timeouts with realistic room for setup and media packaging. Lint YAML/action expressions and test gate cases for failure/skipped/cancelled. Verify an actual hosted run, its complete test counts and uploaded artifact; estimate no speedup until measured. Keep the independent billed workflow separate.` },
  { id: 'Q05', title: 'Create bounded manual live acceptance', goal: 'Verify actual provider behavior and independent session archival.', prompt: `Create a separate main-only workflow_dispatch live-acceptance.yml with reviewed choices course, learning, host, team and all, an explicit confirm_billing input and a restricted live-tests environment. Keep shared and isolated engineering-team credentials separate; store keys in environment secrets and versioned resource IDs in variables. Do not transfer local keys, provision agents/upload skills or run paid sessions merely because a workflow file is created.
Preflight every selected suite before the first billed session: shared settings, immutable skill pins/hashes, isolated Python capability and dedicated team role inventories/versions. Run suites sequentially with no automatic billed retry and stop later suites on failure. Course covers completion, cancel-after-send, deadline and two verified reports in the same retained session then close; learning covers previews, instructions and three skill variants; host covers editor patch/apply plus retained Python and a correlated host tool; team requires independent acceptance, all merged tasks and no unresolved questions.
Validate actual evidence, not exit code zero. Independently retrieve all created sessions to confirm archival, distinguish interrupt acknowledgement from confirmed idle and expose pending cleanup. The reference course uses four, learning six, host three and a successful team commonly five bounded sessions, but actual observed counts must be reported. Keep session budgets, overall role-call caps and execution/cleanup deadlines explicit. Unknown cost/cleanup blocks readiness. Upload authored status/count summaries only; provider payloads and IDs stay private. A live run requires a separate explicit user authorization at execution time.` },
  { id: 'Q06', title: 'Verify live acceptance when explicitly authorized', goal: 'Observe online outcomes without broadening the selected scope.', prompt: `This is an execution checkpoint, not authorization to incur charges. First present the concrete suite selection, configured existing resources, session/reference budget limits and cleanup behavior. If the user has explicitly authorized these billed tests in this session, dispatch the existing bounded live workflow on the exact offline-tested commit; otherwise stop before the paid action and request the missing authorization. Never paste/print keys or provision replacements silently.
Follow the run until its outcomes are known. Download its sanitized summary, require every selected suite to pass and independently confirmed archival of every created session. On a failure, stop later sessions, inspect sanitized diagnostics and reconcile only the run's owned resources. Do not claim configuration readiness is a live pass, automatically increase budgets or blindly resend uncertain work. Record run URL, tested commit, observed scenario counts, cleanup verification and scope limitations. Provider list costs are not final invoices. Verify optional notifications/telemetry separately; these suites do not establish every cloud integration.` },
  { id: 'Q07', title: 'Prepare and publish a verified student release', goal: 'Attach an exact tested package with setup instructions and checksums.', prompt: `Prepare a versioned GitHub release for a concrete commit that passed both required offline CI and the deliberately authorized live acceptance. Download the source ZIP from that successful CI run instead of rebuilding from an untracked local workspace. Independently verify members and distribution-manifest hashes and complete media inventory. Add START-HERE instructions, SHA256SUMS and sanitized live-validation JSON with run URLs and tested commit. Explain offline startup, optional paid setup, student notes/backups and included no-key narration.
Prepare a draft first, compare every uploaded asset's SHA-256 and size with the local checked file, and verify its target commit. Preserve earlier releases/tags rather than retargeting them. Publishing requires the user's authorization; prepare a reviewable draft before asking if it is missing. Once authorized, publish and verify the final release/tag/assets. Do not claim newest main is live-tested if the evidence belongs to an earlier commit. Document real counts and limitations; do not invent a pass, billing total or successful external delivery. Keep release build products/private evidence out of Git.` },
];

export const recoveries = [
  { id: 'R01', title: 'Recover a failed implementation checkpoint', goal: 'Repair the current step without skipping to later features.', prompt: `The current workbook checkpoint failed. Read the exact error, changed files and checkpoint requirements before editing. Reproduce the smallest relevant case. Explain whether the problem is missing implementation, stale test selector, startup/readiness, configuration or an unverified external capability. Fix the cause without deleting acceptance checks, forcing success, overwriting unrelated work or skipping to the next prompt. Preserve the last working checkpoint and show the changed files and exact commands/results. Do not make paid requests or external writes to diagnose an offline failure. If a prerequisite is missing, state the concrete missing capability and keep independent work moving.` },
  { id: 'R02', title: 'Recover a media or timing mismatch', goal: 'Keep narration and red rectangles aligned to their real data.', prompt: `Diagnose this clip/image mismatch using its lesson/kind/step, manifest identity, exact authored text, SHA-256, native image dimensions, WORD timing JSON and DOM/region IDs. Determine whether the wrong asset was copied, the transcript changed, the timestamp source is missing, a gap boundary is wrong or the measured box no longer matches the final image. Fix that identity or mapping; do not fabricate timings, relabel stale audio, draw one whole-section highlight or stretch an arbitrary grid to conceal the mismatch. Preserve valid recordings. Re-recording or image regeneration is a separate authorized author action. Run the specific audit and real playback/seek/gap tests and report evidence.` },
  { id: 'R03', title: 'Audit final feature parity', goal: 'Find concrete omissions before calling the rebuild complete.', prompt: `Compare the rebuilt platform against 11-final-parity-checklist.md and coverage.json in this workbook. Inspect actual code, mounted UI, artifacts and test results. Require every numbered lab and inserted extension, Home/navigation/themes, real source views, guide/player paragraph highlights, 525 lesson clips, notes/backups/quizzes/paths/milestones, 150 original infographics, 150 visual clips, 900 regions, zoom/pan, transcripts/downloads, all audits, explicit live gates and final package/release controls. Verify behaviors rather than matching screenshots or counting placeholder files. Mark each requirement implemented and checked, implemented but unverified, blocked or missing. Repair missing offline capabilities first. Claim a live/provider result only from actual observed authorized execution. Provide the remaining concrete work and never certify bit-for-bit or behavioral equivalence solely because an AI prompt was followed.` },
];
